Apple allocated roughly 80% of its initial iPhone Duo inventory to its retail stores, with locations holding as few as 50 units each, according to <em>Bloomberg</em>'s Mark Gurman. <br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/09/iphone-duo-colors.jpg" alt="" width="1600" height="899" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1063421" /><br/>
Speaking on the latest episode of his <a href="https://omny.fm/shows/power-on-with-mark-gurman/apple-embraces-a-touchscreen-macbook-iphone-duo-crackgate">Power On podcast</a>, Gurman, citing people very close to the device's logistics, said most customers who bought a Duo in store on day one got one by <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/10/23/iphone-duo-long-lines-at-apple-stores/">waiting in line</a>. <br/>
<br/>
Flagship stores were said to have held up to around 250 units, and Fifth Avenue was stocked unusually heavily because Apple executives attended the launch. The inventory split helps explain why <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/10/23/iphone-duo-apple-store-pickup-available-today/">pre-orders slipped to late January</a> within minutes on October 16, while most people who queued on launch day walked away with a new phone.<br/>
<br/>
At the time, Apple described demand as "nothing short of extraordinary" and said it was working to satisfy demand "as quickly as possible," so there's a good chance that, even if a delivery estimate for your order is way out in the future, it could improve over time.<br/>
